# Licence of the verdict database

The code in this repository is MIT (see [LICENSE](LICENSE)). The verdict
database is not, and this file says what it is instead.

## What is covered

Every dump of the shared verdict database published by this project, and any
extract or derivative of one. A dump holds hashes, verdicts, reasons and
source labels. It holds no page text.

## Licence

**Open Database Licence (ODbL) v1.0** — https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/1-0/

In short, and the licence text governs where this summary is loose:

- **Share.** Copy, distribute and use the database freely.
- **Create.** Produce works from it.
- **Adapt.** Modify, transform and build upon it.

On the condition that you:

- **Attribute.** Say where the database came from.
- **Share alike.** Publish any adapted database under ODbL as well.
- **Keep it open.** If you distribute the database in a form that restricts
  access, also make an unrestricted version available.

## Why the code and the data differ

The code is deliberately permissive: it should spread without friction, and a
competitor reimplementing the detector costs this project nothing.

The database is the opposite. It accumulates only because people run the tool,
and it is the one asset that cannot be reproduced by writing better code. Under
a permissive licence a competitor could stand up a rival service on contributed
verdicts in a day, contribute nothing back, and the people who filled the
database would have funded their own replacement. Copyleft keeps improvements
in the commons.

EasyList made the same split for the same reason.

